What you'll find, I think, is that people simply would spend more time 
chasing ways to identify stuff before proceeding.  The reason Rogue had 
less room for caution was that the player was on a harsh food clock in 
Rogue; he COULDN'T hang around waiting for more scrolls of identify.

There is absolutely _no_ clock in Nethack, of course.  I suppose that
technically there's some theoretical limitation on "after you've extincted
all the monsters", but that's far out into the distance beyond
"reasonable/average play" and not worth considering for discussions of
"force the player to act or eventually die" type of thing.

